#{[17592186278287 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3u+2v$ and $y(u,v)=u+3v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =9-2 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278288 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=uv$ and $y=\\frac{v}{u}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}v & u \\\\ -\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{1}{u}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =v\\cdot \\frac{1}{u}\\text{ }-u\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2v}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-2,3)}=\\frac{2(3)}{-2}=-3.\n$$"] [17592186278291 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{u-v}$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(uv).$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u-v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-{e}^{u-v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u-v} & -{e}^{u-v} \\\\ \\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u-v}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)-(-{e}^{u-v})\\cdot \\frac{1}{u} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{u-v}}{v}+\\frac{{e}^{u-v}}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,1)}=\\frac{{e}^{(1)-(1)}}{(1)}+\\frac{{e}^{(1)-(1)}}{(1)}=2.\n$$"] [17592186278292 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{u+v}$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(uv).$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & ={e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)-{e}^{u+v}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{u+v}}{v}-\\frac{{e}^{u+v}}{u} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{u+v}(u-v)}{vu}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278293 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)$ and $y=4u+6v.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =4 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\,\\text{ }4\\, & \\,\\text{ }6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)\\cdot 6-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v))\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278296 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 2v \\\\ 2u & -2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2v)-2v\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-4uv-4uv \\\\  & =-8uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-2,1)}=-8(-2)(1)=16.\n$$"] [17592186278297 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=\\frac{{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}}{2}$ and $y(u,v)=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}u & -v \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =u\\cdot u-(-v)\\cdot v \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-1,-2)}=(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}=5.\n$$"] [17592186278298 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={v}^{2}+uv$ and $y(u,v)=v-u.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v+u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}v & 2v+u \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =v\\cdot 1-(2v+u)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =v+2v+u \\\\  & =3v+u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(8,-2)}=3(-2)+8=2.\n$$"] [17592186278300 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡v-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi /6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(\\frac{\\pi }{6},0)}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186278391 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}u,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+-{1}^{2}+-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={x}^{2}-2yz\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u+v\\,y=u\\,z=v} \\\\  & =(u+v)^{2}-2uv \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{x}^{2}-2yz\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}({u}^{2}+{v}^{2})\\sqrt{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}+u{v}^{2}]}_{u=-1}^{u=1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3}+2{v}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{[\\frac{2}{3}v+\\frac{2}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278393 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =(xy+z)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u\\,y={u}^{2}\\,z=2v} \\\\  & =u.{u}^{2}+2v \\\\  & ={u}^{3}+2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}({u}^{3}+2v)\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278394 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=\\sqrt{8}u.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =({x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =({u}^{2}\\cdot {u}^{2})\\cdot {v}^{2}\\cdot {v}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em} \\\\  & ={u}^{4}{v}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{8}\\hspace{0.167em}{u}^{5}{v}^{4}\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278395 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =(-{x}^{2}+3yz)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u\\,y=u+v\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & =-(u\\cdot u)+3\\cdot (u+v)\\cdot ({v}^{2}) \\\\  & =-{u}^{2}+3(u+v){v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}(-{u}^{2}+3(u+v){v}^{2})\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278411 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=6.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =(x+2y-z)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u-v\\,y=2u+2v\\,z=2v} \\\\  & =(u-v)+2(2u+2v)-(2v) \\\\  & =5u+v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}(5u+v)\\cdot 6\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6{∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}(5u+v)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2}({\\int }_{0}^{1}(5u+v)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{5{u}^{2}}{2}+vu]}_{0}^{1}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{5}{2}+v)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6{[\\frac{5v}{2}+\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =6[5+2] \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278412 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =6xyz\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u\\,y=u+v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =6(u)(u+v)(v) \\\\  & =6({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}6({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{u}^{3}v}{3}+\\frac{{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{v}{3}+\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{v}^{3}}{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278425 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+0+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =xy+z\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u+v\\,y=u-v\\,z=0} \\\\  & =(u+v)(u-v) \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le u\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le v\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(xy+z)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\cdot 2\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}({\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}-{v}^{2}u]}_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{3}-{v}^{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{v}{3}-\\frac{{v}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2[\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{8}{3}] \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278427 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}2v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}-2,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}-2,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =2xz\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u\\,y=u+v\\,z=2v} \\\\  & =2u(2v) \\\\  & =4uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}2xz\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}4uv\\cdot 3\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}4uv\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}2u\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =3\\cdot [{u}^{2}]_{0}^{2}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278442 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨2u,\\hspace{0.222em}-3v,\\hspace{0.222em}4v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-3v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(4v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-3v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(4v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-3,\\hspace{0.222em}4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-8,\\hspace{0.222em}-6⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-8,\\hspace{0.222em}-6⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-8)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+64+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =x(y+z)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=2u\\,y=-3v\\,z=4v} \\\\  & =2u(-3v+4v) \\\\  & =2uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}x(y+z)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}2uv\\cdot 10\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}4uv\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2}2u\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =5\\cdot [{u}^{2}]_{0}^{2}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278443 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨2u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u-2v,\\hspace{0.222em}u+2v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u-2v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u-2v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\hspace{0.222em}-3,\\hspace{0.222em}-6⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨6,\\hspace{0.222em}-3,\\hspace{0.222em}-6⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+9+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{81} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={x}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=2u+v\\,y=2u-2v\\,z=u+2v} \\\\  & =(2u+v)^{2}-(u+2v)^{2} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-1\\le u\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le v\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}(3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2})\\cdot 9\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =9{∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}(3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{2}({\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{2}{[{u}^{3}-3{v}^{2}u]}_{-1}^{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{2}(2-6{v}^{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9\\cdot [2v-2{v}^{3}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-108.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278446 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice we are told that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨-2{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-4uv,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨2x,\\hspace{0.222em}2y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=-uv\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨2{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-2uv,\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨2{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-2uv,\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-2{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-4uv,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}\\cdot (-2{v}^{2})+(-2uv)\\cdot (-4uv)+{v}^{2}\\cdot (-2{u}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+8{u}^{2}{v}^{2}-2{u}^{2}{v}^{2} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}2{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278448 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2v,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={⟨y+z,\\hspace{0.222em}x+z,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩|}_{x=u-v\\,y=2\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨2+{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}u-v+{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨2+{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}u-v+{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2v,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\  & =(2+{v}^{2})\\cdot 0+(u-v+{v}^{2})\\cdot (-2v)+1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-2uv+2{v}^{2}-2{v}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨y+z,\\hspace{0.222em}x+z,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2uv+2{v}^{2}-2{v}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2uv+2{v}^{2}-2{v}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[-{u}^{2}v+2u{v}^{2}-2u{v}^{3}]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-v+2{v}^{2}-2{v}^{3})\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{v}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278449 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-6uv-3{v}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={⟨{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩|}_{x=3u{v}^{2}\\,y=u-v\\,z=2} \\\\  & =⟨{(3u{v}^{2})}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}3u{v}^{2}+u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9{u}^{2}{v}^{4},\\hspace{0.222em}3u{v}^{2}+u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨9{u}^{2}{v}^{4},\\hspace{0.222em}3u{v}^{2}+u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-6uv-3{v}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =9{u}^{2}{v}^{4}\\cdot 0+(3u{v}^{2}+u-v)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-6uv-3{v}^{2}) \\\\  & =-6(2uv+{v}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-6(2uv+{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2uv+{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{[u{v}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{v=0}^{v=1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(u+\\frac{1}{3})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-6{[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}u]}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =-6(-2+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278450 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2u & 0 & v \\\\ 0 & 1 & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & v \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}2u & v \\\\ 0 & u\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-v,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}2u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={⟨3,\\hspace{0.222em}z,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=v\\,z=uv} \\\\  & =⟨3,\\hspace{0.222em}uv,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨3,\\hspace{0.222em}uv,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩\\cdot ⟨-v,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}2u⟩ \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-v)+uv\\cdot (-2{u}^{2})+v\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-3v-2{u}^{3}v+2uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨3,\\hspace{0.222em}z,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-3v-2{u}^{3}v+2uv)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-3v-2{u}^{3}v+2uv)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-3uv-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{4}v+{u}^{2}v]}_{u=-1}^{u=1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-6v)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =[-3{v}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278455 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-2uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2v⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-2uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & -1 & -2v \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2v \\\\ 1 & -2u\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2v \\\\ -1 & -2u\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u+2v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u+2v,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩|}_{x=u-v\\,y=v-u\\,z=-2uv} \\\\  & =⟨u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}v-u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}v-u⟩\\cdot ⟨2u+2v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u+2v,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\  & =(u-v)\\cdot (2u+2v)+0\\cdot (2u+2v)+(v-u)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}-u{v}^{2}]}_{u=0}^{u=1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{3}-{v}^{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{1}{3}v-\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278459 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={⟨-x-z,\\hspace{0.222em}x-y,\\hspace{0.222em}-z⟩|}_{x=u+v\\,y=v-u\\,z=-v} \\\\  & =⟨-(u+v)-(-v),\\hspace{0.222em}u+v-(v-u),\\hspace{0.222em}-(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-u,\\hspace{0.222em}2u,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨-u,\\hspace{0.222em}2u,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩ \\\\  & =(-u)\\cdot 1+2u\\cdot 1+v\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-u+2u+2v \\\\  & =u+2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨-x-z,\\hspace{0.222em}x-y,\\hspace{0.222em}-z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(u+2v)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(u+2v)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[uv+{v}^{2}]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(u+1)\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}+u]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278461 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u{v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-1),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1-2u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u{v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-1),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1-2u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2uv,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ {v}^{2} & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2uv & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}{v}^{2} & -2 \\\\ 2uv & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}{v}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 2uv & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-4uv,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={⟨z,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}-3xy⟩|}_{x=u{v}^{2}\\,y=-1\\,z=1-2u} \\\\  & =⟨1-2u,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}-3(u{v}^{2})(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-2u,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}3u{v}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨1-2u,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}3u{v}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-4uv,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\  & =(1-2u)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-4uv)+3u{v}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-8uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}-3xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-8uv)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}uv\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u{\\int }_{0}^{1}v\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.167em} \\\\  & =-8\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-8\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278462 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =({x}^{2}{z}^{2}+y)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=-u\\,y=-u\\,z=-v} \\\\  & =(-u)^{2}\\cdot (-v)^{2}-u \\\\  & ={u}^{2}{v}^{2}-u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}({u}^{2}{v}^{2}-u)\\sqrt{2}\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278463 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2u,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}2v,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & -2u & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2v & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-2u & 1 \\\\ 2v & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2u \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2u,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2v⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨-2v,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2v⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2v)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(2v)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+4{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =(-x-y-z)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u\\,y={v}^{2}-{u}^{2}\\,z=u} \\\\  & =-u-({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})-u \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}-2u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2}-2u)\\sqrt{8}v\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278464 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨v,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ v & -1 & 0 \\\\ u & 0 & -2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2v\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}v & 0 \\\\ u & -2v\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}v & -1 \\\\ u & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2v,\\hspace{0.222em}2{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨2v,\\hspace{0.222em}2{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2v)^{2}+(2{v}^{2})^{2}+(u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+{u}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =(x-{y}^{2}z)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=uv\\,y=-u\\,z=-{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =uv-(-u)^{2}\\cdot -{v}^{2} \\\\  & =uv+{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}(uv+{u}^{2}{v}^{2})\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+{u}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278470 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}+2v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{3}+3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}2u,\\hspace{0.222em}3{u}^{2}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}+2v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{3}+3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 2u & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2u \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6u-6{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}3{u}^{2}-3,\\hspace{0.222em}2-2u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩{|}_{x=u+v\\,y={u}^{2}+2v\\,z={u}^{3}+3v} \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\\cdot ⟨6u-6{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}3{u}^{2}-3,\\hspace{0.222em}2-2u⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot (6u-6{u}^{2})+0\\cdot (3{u}^{2}-3)+2\\cdot (2-2u) \\\\  & =6u-6{u}^{2}+4-4u \\\\  & =-6{u}^{2}+2u+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-6)\\cdot {u}^{2}+2\\cdot u+4\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=-6+2+4=0.\n$$"] [17592186278471 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u{e}^{v}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u{e}^{-v}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{v},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{-v},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u{e}^{v}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u{e}^{-v}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u{e}^{v},\\hspace{0.222em}-u{e}^{-v},\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ {e}^{v} & {e}^{-v} & 1 \\\\ u{e}^{v} & -u{e}^{-v} & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{-v} & 1 \\\\ -u{e}^{-v} & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{v} & 1 \\\\ u{e}^{v} & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{v} & {e}^{-v} \\\\ u{e}^{v} & -u{e}^{-v}\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u{e}^{-v},\\hspace{0.222em}u{e}^{v},\\hspace{0.222em}-2u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨x-\\frac{1}{z},\\hspace{0.222em}y+\\frac{1}{z},\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩{|}_{x=u{e}^{v}\\,y=u{e}^{-v}\\,z=u} \\\\  & =⟨u{e}^{v}-\\frac{1}{u},\\hspace{0.222em}u{e}^{-v}+\\frac{1}{u},\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨u{e}^{v}-\\frac{1}{u},\\hspace{0.222em}u{e}^{-v}+\\frac{1}{u},\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩\\cdot ⟨u{e}^{-v},\\hspace{0.222em}u{e}^{v},\\hspace{0.222em}-2u⟩ \\\\  & =(u{e}^{v}-\\frac{1}{u})\\cdot u{e}^{-v}+(u{e}^{-v}+\\frac{1}{u})\\cdot u{e}^{v}+u\\cdot (-2u) \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-{e}^{-v}+{u}^{2}+{e}^{v}-2{u}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{v}-{e}^{-v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1\\cdot {e}^{v}+(-1)\\cdot {e}^{-v}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=1-1=0.\n$$"] [17592186278507 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice we are told that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2u⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨{z}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}x⟩{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=v\\,z=u} \\\\  & =⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2u⟩ \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot (-1)+{v}^{2}\\cdot 0+{u}^{2}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{u}^{3}-{u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}2{u}^{3}-{u}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186281325 "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\